    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$3.80 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for rendang that a Malaysian restaurant would charge $16 for, if you can even find one nearby. Fourteen restaurant-grade dinners from one pot.",
    "shop_smart":  "Chicken breast in the 6 pound pack is a strong per-pound deal on our board, and that low price is the whole reason this dish lands where it does.\nThe lemongrass paste tube looks expensive but replaces a specialty store trip, keeps for months in the fridge, and covers this whole batch with room to spare.\nOnions are the biggest produce line here, so buy the big bag. Six pounds bought in a bag runs roughly half the per-pound cost of buying them loose.",
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Blitz the onions, garlic, ginger, and lemongrass paste into a rough puree in a blender or food processor, working in batches.",
                    "Heat the vegetable oil in your big pot over medium and fry the puree 10 to 15 minutes, stirring often, until it darkens and the raw onion smell cooks off.",
                    "Stir in the cinnamon, cloves, garam masala, and red pepper flakes and cook 1 minute until fragrant.",
                    "Add the cubed chicken breast and stir to coat, then pour in the coconut milk and sugar and bring to a simmer.",
                    "Simmer uncovered 35 to 45 minutes, stirring more often near the end, until the sauce reduces to a thick, deep brown coat on the chicken. Confirm the chicken reads 165 F.",
                    "Season with salt and pepper. The sauce should cling, not pool."
                ],
